        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Serum phosphorus concentration<2.5 mg/dL.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Control of phosphorus is influenced by the actions and interactions of PTH and vitamin D with the gastrointestinal tract, bone, kidneys, and parathyroid glands.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Decreased serum phosphorus concentration can be caused by translocation of phosphorus from the extracellular fluid into cells, decreased renal reabsorption, or decreased intestinal absorption of phosphorus.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Low serum phosphorus can lead to adenosine triphosphate (ATP) depletion, which affects cells with high ATP energy demands (skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, nerve tissue, and red blood cells).",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Many important enzyme systems are dependent on adequate phosphorus levels, including glycolysis, ammoniagenesis, 1-hydroxylation of 25(OH)-cholecalciferol, and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, which are essential for energy production, acid excretion, calcium balance, and tissue oxygenation, respectively.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phosphorus is also required for mainten ance of cell membrane integrity, playing an essential role in the production of ATP, guanosine triphosphate, cyclic adenosine monophosphate (AMP), and phosphocreatinine.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diabetic (ketototic and nonketotic) patients are at increased risk for hypophosphatemia due to depleted phosphorus stores, lost muscle mass, and urinary losses. Insulin administration yields ATP from glycolysis, causing translocation of",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemic/lymphatic/immune—hemolysis, impaired oxygen delivery to tissues, impaired leukocyte and platelet function.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neurologic—impaired glucose uptake leads to encephalopathy, seizures, and coma.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Musculoskeletal—rhabdomyolysis, weakness, pain, ventilatory failure, and gastrointestinal ileus.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiac—impaired contractility.",
              "pdf_page": 821,
              "printed_page": 739,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
